+
+### iPhone Duo portrait and tabletop layout
+
+- **Symptom:** portrait gameplay overlaps the touch controls, or touch coordinates land outside the game picture.
+- **Cause:** the frontend assumed a landscape picture with controls overlaid across the full view, so its video and touch geometry did not follow the portrait layout. A native rotation A/B on the iPhone Duo iOS 27.1 simulator also showed that deprecated `UIRequiresFullScreen` blocked upside-down portrait despite all four orientations being declared; without the key, UIKit applied all four.
+- **Fix:** remove fullscreen compatibility and let UIKit handle rotation. The phone orientation list stays landscape-only for ordinary iPhones and the Duo outer display; UIKit ignores that list on the Duo inner display, where layout follows current window bounds. The iPad list allows all four orientations for resizable windows. Portrait places an aspect-fitted 16:9 game view above the controls, using a valid native horizontal division when available and the midpoint otherwise. Landscape keeps the overlay unless UIKit reports a partially open hinge with a valid horizontal division. Local-game layout changes do not request scene rotation. On Duo in portrait, only the top safe-area inset is applied to video and controls; left, right and bottom are ignored. Other devices and landscape keep their existing insets.
+- **Input:** game touches map through the actual game-view rectangle. When virtual controls are visible, touches in the controls pane go to the gamepad; layout changes release active virtual touches.
+- **Check:** [Duo harness notes](../../dm/ios/tests/duo/README.md) describe layout and input coverage. Synthetic checks do not establish physical pose, rotation, rendering, or controller acceptance. The operator-driven native rotation test checks bounds and portrait game-touch routing while the operator cycles through four device poses. The owner validated build 1.3.19 on a physical Duo: the outer display remains landscape-only and the inner display retains both portrait directions, landscape and tabletop layouts. Native iPad window-resizing checks exercise the production presentation and input path with synthetic Metal frames; full-game testing on a physical iPad remains outstanding.
+- **Found on:** [Pokémon Brilliant Diamond](../../dm/games/0100000011D90000/README.md).
